          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Finance is the strictest niche in publishing. Editors are cautious, readers are sceptical, and Google holds money content to its highest quality bar.</p>

            <p>That works in your favour if you have something real to say. A generic "5 budgeting tips" post gets deleted. A piece backed by a credential, a dataset or genuine market experience gets read.</p>

            <p>The list of 100 is below. First, narrow it to the slice that fits what you can actually write.</p>

            <h2 id="subniche">Find your finance sub-niche first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Your sub-niche</th><th>Best fit on this list</th><th>What they run</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Market news &amp; analysis</td><td>Bloomberg, CNBC, MarketWatch, Barron's, Seeking Alpha, Benzinga</td><td>Analyst commentary, earnings takes, macro views</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Personal finance</td><td>NerdWallet, Bankrate, The Penny Hoarder, Investopedia, GOBankingRates</td><td>How-to guides, product explainers, money habits</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Investing &amp; retirement</td><td>Morningstar, Kiplinger, The Motley Fool, White Coat Investor, ChooseFI</td><td>Portfolio strategy, tax, FIRE, evidence-based investing</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Fintech</td><td>Finextra, PYMNTS, The Fintech Times, AltFi, Tearsheet</td><td>Industry news, product launches, regulation, data</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Crypto</td><td>CoinDesk, Cointelegraph, Decrypt, The Tokenist</td><td>Market coverage, protocol explainers, regulation</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Small business finance</td><td>Entrepreneur, Inc, SCORE, Nav, Small Business Trends</td><td>Cash flow, funding, accounting, growth</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>UK personal finance</td><td>This Is Money, MoneyWeek, Money Saving Expert, lovemoney, Citywire</td><td>UK tax, pensions, savings, deals</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> in finance, credibility is the currency. Editors care more about who you are and whether the claims hold up than about your DR. A licensed adviser or a solid dataset opens doors that outreach volume never will.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 finance sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for finance editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with your credential or your data.</strong> "I'm a CFP" or "we analysed 10,000 anonymised transactions" earns the read in the first line.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch a specific, checkable claim.</strong> Not "how to save money". Something like "the average saver loses X to this one fee", with the working.</li>
              <li><strong>Be compliance-aware.</strong> Frame it as education, not advice. Avoid predictions dressed as certainties. Editors in this niche have a legal team.</li>
              <li><strong>Match their format and house style.</strong> News outlets want tight analysis. Personal-finance sites want structured how-tos.</li>
              <li><strong>One strong clip</strong> in a similar register.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any product or firm you represent</strong> up front. Non-negotiable in finance.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> could a compliance officer read this piece and approve it as neutral education? If yes, pitch it. If it nudges readers toward your product, an editor will see it and pass.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Give specific investment or tax advice without the credentials or disclaimers to back it.</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate links to brokers, cards or loans, or point exact-match anchors at your product's signup page.</li>
              <li>Make return predictions or "guaranteed" claims. Fast track to rejection, and worse.</li>
              <li>Send the same pitch across sub-niches. A crypto editor and a retirement-planning editor want opposite things.</li>
              <li>Chase the DR 90 news titles first. Start where a credentialed contributor can realistically land a byline.</li>
            </ul>
